{
  "_source": "USGS Watershed Boundary Dataset, six-digit hydrologic units (layer 3), queried at each town's own coordinates: https://hydro.nationalmap.gov/arcgis/rest/services/wbd/MapServer/3/query?geometry=<lon>,<lat>&geometryType=esriGeometryPoint&inSR=4326&outFields=huc6,name&returnGeometry=false&f=json (read 11 November 2026). Every US town with a stored watershed record (67).",
  "_note": "src/lib/ground.ts prefers these codes and names over the stored ws:v1 records, whose writer is not in this repository. Audit of 11 November 2026: the stored code was wrong at Pittsburgh (050100 Allegheny; the point is in 050200 Monongahela) and Brownsville (130900; 121102), and the query found five more (Fairbanks, Burlington, Duluth, Kansas City, Memphis); `stored` is the code the record held. Where the stored code was in another two-digit region, `reviewed` says why the stored river and system stand, or `river` and `system` replace them.",
  "_oceans": "Audit of 13 November 2026 (Day 4): the ocean was the stored record's and nothing checked it (Fort Myers and Orlando read \"drains to the Atlantic Ocean\"). Each row now carries `ocean`, from its USGS water resources region: 01, 02 the Atlantic; 04 the Great Lakes and the St. Lawrence, so the Atlantic (USGS region definitions, https://water.usgs.gov/GIS/huc_name.html, read 13 November 2026); 05 to 08, 10 and 11 the Mississippi system, so the Gulf of Mexico; 09 Lake Winnipeg and Hudson Bay; 12 and 13 the Gulf of Mexico; 15 the Gulf of California; 17 and 18 the Pacific (regions 08 to 18 as the USGS definitions are quoted at https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Water_resource_region, read 13 November 2026); 19 (Alaska) keeps the stored Pacific Ocean, no source read. Region 03 (South Atlantic-Gulf) discharges to both coasts: subregions 0301 to 0308 are coastal drainage to the Atlantic, ending at St. Lucie Inlet, and 0310 to 0318 to the Gulf (USGS, as above). Subregion 0309 (Southern Florida) runs from St. Lucie Inlet round to the Caloosahatchee, so each 0309 town carries `oceanCheck`: its eight-digit unit from the USGS Watershed Boundary Dataset (layer 4) at the point, and the sources read.",
